===Verb===
{{en-verb|overruns|overrunning|overran|overrun}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[defeat]] an [[enemy]] and [[invade]] in great [[number]]s, [[seize|seizing]] the enemy [[position]]s [[conclusively]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[infest]], [[swarm]] over, [[flow]] over.
#: {{ux|en|The vine '''overran''' its trellis; the field is '''overrun''' with weeds.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Ireland|passage=those barbarous nations that '''over-ran''' the world}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=November 7, 2012|author=Matt Bai|title=Winning a Second Term, Obama Will Confront Familiar Headwinds|work=New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/us/politics/in-president-obamas-second-term-familiar-challenges.html?pagewanted=all&_r=0| passage=As President Obama turns his attention once again to filling out a cabinet and writing an Inaugural Address, this much is clear: he should not expect to bask in a surge of national unity, or to witness a crowd of millions '''overrun''' the Mall just to say they were there.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[run]] [[past]]; to run beyond.
#: {{ux|en|The athlete '''overran''' the finish line and kept going.}}
#: {{ux|en|One line '''overruns''' another in length.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|2 Samuel|18|23|passage=Ahimaaz ran by the way of the plain, and '''overran''' Cushi.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=March|title=Talking of trains|journal=Trains Illustrated| page=138| text=The collision occurred when an up empty electric train travelling on the up main through line '''overran''' the inner home signal and rammed the side of the departing 6.14 p.m. Waterloo-Weymouth steam train, which was leaving platform 12 for the down main through line.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To run past the [[end]] of.
##: {{ux|en|The aircraft didn't stop in time and '''overran''' the runway.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[continue]] for too long.
#: ''The performance '''overran''' by ten minutes, which caused some people to miss their bus home.''
# {{lb|en|printing}} To [[carry]] (some [[type]], a [[line]] or [[column]], etc.) [[backward]] or [[forward]] into an [[adjacent]] line or [[page]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[go beyond]]; to [[extend]] in part beyond.
#: ''In machinery, a sliding piece is said to '''overrun''' its bearing when its forward end goes beyond it.''
#* {{quote-web|en|date=9 Jun 24|author={{w|maia arson crimew}}|work=maia blog|url=https://maia.crimew.gay/posts/fuckstalkerware-7/|title=israeli national police found trying to purchase stalkerware|text=all of these services, including mSpy, function with the same kind of predatory subscription scheme, luring you in with free trials that autorenew as abhorrently expensive monthly or yearly subscriptions. canceling the service requires reaching out to support, so users are typically unable to avoid '''overrunning''' the trial.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[abuse]] or [[oppress]], as if by [[tread]]ing upon.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=V|canto=II|stanza=9|passage=None of them the feeble '''overren'''.}}

